/* book Worked example: Q: Water drops of 100 µm settle through an organic liquid (Δρ = 200 kg/m³, μc = 1.0 mPa·s). Find the settling velocity under gravity and in a centrifugal extractor turning at 3000 rpm at r = 0.20 m. How long does each take to cross 0.30 m of a gravity settler and 10 mm of the rotor?
   1. Gravity: ut = 200 × 9.81 × (1.0 × 10⁻⁴)²/(18 × 10⁻³) = 1.09 × 10⁻³ m/s.
   2. ω = 2π × 3000/60 = 314.2 rad/s; ω²r = 314.2² × 0.20 = 19 740 m/s², which is 19 740/9.81 = 2012 g.
   3. Centrifugal: uc = 2012 × 1.09 × 10⁻³ = 2.19 m/s (Stokes' law is only a rough guide at this speed).
   4. Times: gravity 0.30/1.09 × 10⁻³ = 275 s, about 4.6 min; centrifuge 0.010/2.19 = 0.0046 s.
   Answer: 1.09 mm/s under gravity against about 2.2 m/s at 2012 g; separation drops from minutes to milliseconds. */
